1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,488
Sweetheart, every Christmas Day of my whole

2
00:00:03,488 --> 00:00:05,354
life, at three o'clock,

3
00:00:05,354 --> 00:00:06,895
right after dinner,

4
00:00:06,895 --> 00:00:09,492
my mother put on her oldest coat

5
00:00:09,492 --> 00:00:11,844
and a scarf with holes in it,

6
00:00:11,844 --> 00:00:13,386
and left the house.

7
00:00:13,566 --> 00:00:15,441
She said she was helping at the

8
00:00:15,441 --> 00:00:15,865
church.

9
00:00:16,045 --> 00:00:17,647
She came home at dark.

10
00:00:17,827 --> 00:00:19,684
For thirty-five years.

11
00:00:19,864 --> 00:00:22,850
Nobody ever asked her where she really

12
00:00:22,850 --> 00:00:23,243
went.

13
00:00:23,423 --> 00:00:25,970
And then, at her funeral in nineteen

14
00:00:25,970 --> 00:00:28,941
eighty-nine, the back of the church filled

15
00:00:28,941 --> 00:00:30,215
up with strangers.

16
00:00:30,564 --> 00:00:32,905
Tonight I'm going to tell you who

17
00:00:32,905 --> 00:00:34,395
those strangers were,

18
00:00:34,395 --> 00:00:37,445
where my mother really went every Christmas

19
00:00:37,445 --> 00:00:40,282
afternoon, and the reason she never told

20
00:00:40,282 --> 00:00:42,198
a soul, not even my father.

21
00:00:42,378 --> 00:00:43,305
Stay with me,

22
00:00:43,305 --> 00:00:45,587
honey, because it goes back to a

23
00:00:45,587 --> 00:00:48,083
soup line in the winter of nineteen

24
00:00:48,083 --> 00:00:50,865
thirty-two, when she was a little girl.

25
00:00:51,215 --> 00:00:54,086
Christmas at our house on Elm Street

26
00:00:54,086 --> 00:00:55,682
was always the same,

27
00:00:55,682 --> 00:00:56,160
honey.

28
00:00:56,340 --> 00:00:57,722
Church in the morning.

29
00:00:57,902 --> 00:00:58,784
Presents.

30
00:00:58,964 --> 00:01:00,215
Dinner at one,

31
00:01:00,215 --> 00:01:03,611
ham and sweet potatoes and my mother's

32
00:01:03,611 --> 00:01:03,968
pie.

33
00:01:04,148 --> 00:01:06,099
And then, at three o'clock,

34
00:01:06,099 --> 00:01:08,557
while my father fell asleep in his

35
00:01:08,557 --> 00:01:10,941
chair and Danny and I played with

36
00:01:10,941 --> 00:01:12,026
our new things,

37
00:01:12,026 --> 00:01:14,194
my mother would go to the hall

38
00:01:14,194 --> 00:01:16,651
closet and take out that old brown

39
00:01:16,651 --> 00:01:17,012
coat.

40
00:01:17,192 --> 00:01:19,612
The one with the torn pocket she'd

41
00:01:19,612 --> 00:01:20,466
never fixed.

42
00:01:20,816 --> 00:01:22,329
She had a good coat,

43
00:01:22,329 --> 00:01:23,161
sweetheart.

44
00:01:23,341 --> 00:01:25,611
A navy wool one my father bought

45
00:01:25,611 --> 00:01:25,895
her.

46
00:01:26,075 --> 00:01:28,269
But on Christmas afternoon,

47
00:01:28,269 --> 00:01:30,545
it was always the brown one,

48
00:01:30,545 --> 00:01:33,064
and an old gray scarf with moth

49
00:01:33,064 --> 00:01:34,933
holes, and no lipstick.

50
00:01:35,113 --> 00:01:36,513
I asked her once,

51
00:01:36,513 --> 00:01:38,242
when I was about ten,

52
00:01:38,242 --> 00:01:40,466
why she wore the ugly coat.

53
00:01:40,646 --> 00:01:41,610
And she said,

54
00:01:41,610 --> 00:01:42,946
because it's warm,

55
00:01:42,946 --> 00:01:45,766
Ruthie, and the church hall is drafty.

56
00:01:45,946 --> 00:01:48,195
And she kissed my head and went

57
00:01:48,195 --> 00:01:49,138
out the door.

58
00:01:49,318 --> 00:01:51,448
In a minute I'll tell you what

59
00:01:51,448 --> 00:01:53,010
I saw from the window.

60
00:01:53,360 --> 00:01:56,032
I watched her walk down Elm Street,

61
00:01:56,032 --> 00:01:57,482
honey, in the snow.

62
00:01:57,662 --> 00:01:59,607
But she didn't turn left,

63
00:01:59,607 --> 00:02:01,319
toward First Lutheran.

64
00:02:01,499 --> 00:02:03,126
She turned right,

65
00:02:03,126 --> 00:02:04,657
toward downtown.

66
00:02:04,837 --> 00:02:06,970
I thought maybe she was going to

67
00:02:06,970 --> 00:02:08,169
visit a sick lady.

68
00:02:08,349 --> 00:02:10,509
I didn't think much more about it.

69
00:02:10,689 --> 00:02:11,838
Children don't.

70
00:02:12,018 --> 00:02:14,285
And every year after that,

71
00:02:14,285 --> 00:02:16,902
three o'clock, the brown coat,

72
00:02:16,902 --> 00:02:18,210
the gray scarf,

73
00:02:18,210 --> 00:02:19,954
and home after dark,

74
00:02:19,954 --> 00:02:22,309
cold, with her cheeks pink,

75
00:02:22,309 --> 00:02:23,268
very quiet.

76
00:02:23,618 --> 00:02:25,426
Danny had a theory,

77
00:02:25,426 --> 00:02:25,998
honey.

78
00:02:26,178 --> 00:02:27,554
When he was twelve,

79
00:02:27,554 --> 00:02:30,016
he decided our mother had a secret

80
00:02:30,016 --> 00:02:30,740
boyfriend.

81
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:32,859
He told me so in a whisper

82
00:02:32,859 --> 00:02:34,126
under the covers,

83
00:02:34,126 --> 00:02:35,170
very dramatic.

84
00:02:35,350 --> 00:02:36,622
I said, Danny,

85
00:02:36,622 --> 00:02:40,168
she's wearing the ugliest coat in Ohio.

86
00:02:40,348 --> 00:02:42,496
Who's she going to meet in that?

87
00:02:42,676 --> 00:02:45,360
He said, that's the disguise,

88
00:02:45,360 --> 00:02:46,008
Ruthie.

89
00:02:46,188 --> 00:02:48,608
We laughed so hard my father banged

90
00:02:48,608 --> 00:02:49,438
on the wall.

91
00:02:49,618 --> 00:02:51,739
I'll tell you how close to the

92
00:02:51,739 --> 00:02:54,355
truth Danny actually was in a minute.

93
00:02:54,705 --> 00:02:57,300
When I grew up and married Harold,

94
00:02:57,300 --> 00:03:00,200
honey, we had Christmas at my parents'

95
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:01,497
house every year.

96
00:03:01,677 --> 00:03:02,896
And every year,

97
00:03:02,896 --> 00:03:04,278
at three o'clock,

98
00:03:04,278 --> 00:03:06,960
my mother put on that brown coat.

99
00:03:07,140 --> 00:03:09,555
Harold asked about it once.

100
00:03:09,735 --> 00:03:12,417
My father just shook his head and

101
00:03:12,417 --> 00:03:15,180
said, Dorothy has her church work,

102
00:03:15,180 --> 00:03:17,455
and I've learned not to ask.

103
00:03:17,635 --> 00:03:19,992
My father was a patient man.

104
00:03:20,172 --> 00:03:22,425
He trusted her completely.

105
00:03:22,605 --> 00:03:24,532
And she never explained.

106
00:03:24,712 --> 00:03:27,719
Not once, in thirty-five years.

107
00:03:28,068 --> 00:03:31,366
If Grandma's story is touching your heart,

108
00:03:31,366 --> 00:03:33,486
tap the like button for me,

109
00:03:33,486 --> 00:03:34,349
sweetheart.

110
00:03:34,529 --> 00:03:37,676
It helps more people find these stories.

111
00:03:37,856 --> 00:03:40,631
My father passed in nineteen eighty-four.

112
00:03:40,811 --> 00:03:43,572
And that first Christmas without him,

113
00:03:43,572 --> 00:03:46,035
I thought surely she'd stay home.

114
00:03:46,215 --> 00:03:47,694
She was sixty-four,

115
00:03:47,694 --> 00:03:50,573
and grieving, and it was nine degrees

116
00:03:50,573 --> 00:03:51,196
outside.

117
00:03:51,376 --> 00:03:52,873
But at three o'clock,

118
00:03:52,873 --> 00:03:54,870
she stood up from the table.

119
00:03:55,050 --> 00:03:56,231
And I said,

120
00:03:56,231 --> 00:03:59,021
Mother, please, not today.

121
00:03:59,201 --> 00:04:00,460
And she said,

122
00:04:00,460 --> 00:04:02,881
especially today, Ruthie.

123
00:04:03,061 --> 00:04:04,129
And she went.

124
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:07,042
My mother passed in March of nineteen

125
00:04:07,042 --> 00:04:08,357
eighty-nine, honey.

126
00:04:08,537 --> 00:04:10,759
Her heart, in her sleep,

127
00:04:10,759 --> 00:04:12,148
in her own bed.

128
00:04:12,328 --> 00:04:13,884
She was sixty-nine.

129
00:04:14,064 --> 00:04:16,857
The funeral was at First Lutheran,

130
00:04:16,857 --> 00:04:19,733
and we expected maybe sixty people,

131
00:04:19,733 --> 00:04:21,540
family, church ladies,

132
00:04:21,540 --> 00:04:23,677
neighbors from Elm Street.

133
00:04:23,857 --> 00:04:26,074
And it was all those people.

134
00:04:26,254 --> 00:04:28,787
But as the service was starting,

135
00:04:28,787 --> 00:04:31,003
the back doors kept opening.

136
00:04:31,183 --> 00:04:33,284
And men kept coming in.

137
00:04:33,464 --> 00:04:34,950
One at a time.

138
00:04:35,130 --> 00:04:37,359
And sitting in the back pews.

139
00:04:37,710 --> 00:04:39,336
They were old men,

140
00:04:39,336 --> 00:04:42,679
mostly, sweetheart, and some younger.

141
00:04:42,859 --> 00:04:44,334
In worn-out coats,

142
00:04:44,334 --> 00:04:45,563
and work boots,

143
00:04:45,563 --> 00:04:48,104
and knit caps they took off and

144
00:04:48,104 --> 00:04:49,743
held in their hands.

145
00:04:49,923 --> 00:04:51,793
Some of them hadn't shaved.

146
00:04:51,973 --> 00:04:53,884
One had a cane made from a

147
00:04:53,884 --> 00:04:54,840
broom handle.

148
00:04:55,020 --> 00:04:56,878
They didn't know anybody.

149
00:04:57,058 --> 00:04:59,124
They didn't sit with anybody.

150
00:04:59,304 --> 00:05:01,442
They filled the last four pews,

151
00:05:01,442 --> 00:05:03,855
and they stood along the back wall.

152
00:05:04,035 --> 00:05:05,900
By the time the pastor started,

153
00:05:05,900 --> 00:05:08,006
there were more than forty of them.

154
00:05:08,356 --> 00:05:10,842
The church ladies were whispering,

155
00:05:10,842 --> 00:05:11,281
honey.

156
00:05:11,461 --> 00:05:12,541
Who are they?

157
00:05:12,721 --> 00:05:14,370
Are they in the right church?

158
00:05:14,550 --> 00:05:16,454
And I didn't know either.

159
00:05:16,634 --> 00:05:18,675
I sat in the front pew with

160
00:05:18,675 --> 00:05:19,961
Harold and Danny,

161
00:05:19,961 --> 00:05:22,531
and I kept turning around to look.

162
00:05:22,711 --> 00:05:25,691
They sat through the whole service,

163
00:05:25,691 --> 00:05:28,075
very quiet, very respectful.

164
00:05:28,255 --> 00:05:29,881
Some of them were crying.

165
00:05:30,061 --> 00:05:31,736
And when it was over,

166
00:05:31,736 --> 00:05:33,570
they came up the aisle,

167
00:05:33,570 --> 00:05:34,447
one by one,

168
00:05:34,447 --> 00:05:35,564
to the casket.

169
00:05:35,744 --> 00:05:36,967
Stay with me,

170
00:05:36,967 --> 00:05:37,532
honey.

171
00:05:37,881 --> 00:05:40,144
Danny leaned over to me in the

172
00:05:40,144 --> 00:05:41,501
pew and whispered,

173
00:05:41,501 --> 00:05:43,989
Ruthie, I think I was wrong about

174
00:05:43,989 --> 00:05:45,045
the boyfriend.

175
00:05:45,225 --> 00:05:47,775
And I'm ashamed to say I laughed,

176
00:05:47,775 --> 00:05:49,861
at my own mother's funeral,

177
00:05:49,861 --> 00:05:51,175
and then I cried,

178
00:05:51,175 --> 00:05:53,725
because I didn't know what any of

179
00:05:53,725 --> 00:05:54,420
it meant.

180
00:05:54,600 --> 00:05:56,736
Harold squeezed my hand.

181
00:05:56,916 --> 00:05:58,248
He said, quietly,

182
00:05:58,248 --> 00:06:00,990
Ruthie, I think we're about to find

183
00:06:00,990 --> 00:06:03,731
out where she went every Christmas.

184
00:06:03,911 --> 00:06:06,245
He always was the smart one.

185
00:06:06,595 --> 00:06:08,366
So far, the brown coat,

186
00:06:08,366 --> 00:06:10,678
three o'clock every Christmas,

187
00:06:10,678 --> 00:06:12,372
the turn to the right,

188
00:06:12,372 --> 00:06:15,685
and forty strangers at my mother's funeral.

189
00:06:15,865 --> 00:06:18,248
The last one up the aisle was

190
00:06:18,248 --> 00:06:19,646
a tall, thin man,

191
00:06:19,646 --> 00:06:22,604
about seventy, with a gray beard and

192
00:06:22,604 --> 00:06:23,179
a cane.

193
00:06:23,359 --> 00:06:25,696
He stood by the casket a long

194
00:06:25,696 --> 00:06:26,099
time.

195
00:06:26,279 --> 00:06:28,620
Then he came over to the front

196
00:06:28,620 --> 00:06:30,570
pew, and he looked at me,

197
00:06:30,570 --> 00:06:31,506
and he said,

198
00:06:31,506 --> 00:06:32,676
are you Ruthie?

199
00:06:32,856 --> 00:06:34,440
And I said,

200
00:06:34,440 --> 00:06:35,016
yes.

201
00:06:35,196 --> 00:06:36,148
Who are you?

202
00:06:36,498 --> 00:06:38,629
He said, my name is Eddie,

203
00:06:38,629 --> 00:06:39,121
ma'am.

204
00:06:39,301 --> 00:06:41,785
Your mother ate Christmas dinner with me

205
00:06:41,785 --> 00:06:43,771
every year for thirty-one years.

206
00:06:43,951 --> 00:06:44,868
At the St.

207
00:06:45,048 --> 00:06:47,974
Vincent's soup kitchen on Fourth Street.

208
00:06:48,154 --> 00:06:50,532
And I just stared at him,

209
00:06:50,532 --> 00:06:51,103
honey.

210
00:06:51,283 --> 00:06:52,305
And he said,

211
00:06:52,305 --> 00:06:53,922
she never told you,

212
00:06:53,922 --> 00:06:54,603
did she?

213
00:06:54,783 --> 00:06:56,119
She said she wouldn't.

214
00:06:56,299 --> 00:06:58,944
She said if anybody knew she came,

215
00:06:58,944 --> 00:07:01,435
they'd think she was there to do

216
00:07:01,435 --> 00:07:02,057
charity.

217
00:07:02,237 --> 00:07:04,481
And she didn't want us to feel

218
00:07:04,481 --> 00:07:05,453
like charity.

219
00:07:05,803 --> 00:07:07,812
Eddie told me everything,

220
00:07:07,812 --> 00:07:10,865
honey, standing by my mother's casket.

221
00:07:11,045 --> 00:07:13,796
She came every Christmas at three-thirty,

222
00:07:13,796 --> 00:07:16,279
when the volunteers had gone home and

223
00:07:16,279 --> 00:07:18,023
the hall was emptying out.

224
00:07:18,203 --> 00:07:21,117
In her brown coat and gray scarf.

225
00:07:21,297 --> 00:07:23,203
She'd go through the line,

226
00:07:23,203 --> 00:07:24,376
and get a plate,

227
00:07:24,376 --> 00:07:26,208
just like everybody else.

228
00:07:26,388 --> 00:07:28,268
And she'd sit down at a table

229
00:07:28,268 --> 00:07:30,278
with whoever was sitting alone.

230
00:07:30,458 --> 00:07:32,344
And she'd eat with them,

231
00:07:32,344 --> 00:07:33,838
and talk with them,

232
00:07:33,838 --> 00:07:34,939
for two hours.

233
00:07:35,288 --> 00:07:37,296
She knew all their names,

234
00:07:37,296 --> 00:07:38,179
sweetheart.

235
00:07:38,359 --> 00:07:40,602
She asked about their families,

236
00:07:40,602 --> 00:07:41,687
their old jobs,

237
00:07:41,687 --> 00:07:43,061
where they grew up.

238
00:07:43,241 --> 00:07:45,401
She remembered from year to year.

239
00:07:45,581 --> 00:07:46,869
She brought a pie,

240
00:07:46,869 --> 00:07:48,802
every year, in a plain tin,

241
00:07:48,802 --> 00:07:51,020
and set it on the dessert table

242
00:07:51,020 --> 00:07:52,094
without a word.

243
00:07:52,274 --> 00:07:55,095
Eddie said, the volunteers were kind.

244
00:07:55,275 --> 00:07:57,353
But they stood behind the counter.

245
00:07:57,533 --> 00:07:59,832
Your mother sat down at the table.

246
00:08:00,012 --> 00:08:01,719
She ate what we ate.

247
00:08:01,899 --> 00:08:03,896
That's why we came today.

248
00:08:04,245 --> 00:08:07,019
Eddie said, the first year I met

249
00:08:07,019 --> 00:08:09,272
her, nineteen fifty-eight,

250
00:08:09,272 --> 00:08:11,699
I'd lost my job and my wife.

251
00:08:11,879 --> 00:08:13,806
I was going to end it that

252
00:08:13,806 --> 00:08:14,770
night, ma'am.

253
00:08:14,950 --> 00:08:16,088
I'd decided.

254
00:08:16,268 --> 00:08:18,395
And this lady in a brown coat

255
00:08:18,395 --> 00:08:20,816
sat down across from me and asked

256
00:08:20,816 --> 00:08:23,310
me where I learned to fix engines,

257
00:08:23,310 --> 00:08:25,950
because I had grease under my nails.

258
00:08:26,130 --> 00:08:27,686
We talked till dark.

259
00:08:27,866 --> 00:08:29,526
And I went home.

260
00:08:29,706 --> 00:08:32,063
And I came back the next year,

261
00:08:32,063 --> 00:08:34,734
because I wanted to see her again.

262
00:08:35,083 --> 00:08:37,402
He said, she came to my wedding,

263
00:08:37,402 --> 00:08:39,577
ma'am, in nineteen sixty-four.

264
00:08:39,757 --> 00:08:41,662
I met a good woman at the

265
00:08:41,662 --> 00:08:43,644
soup kitchen, a volunteer,

266
00:08:43,644 --> 00:08:45,092
and we got married,

267
00:08:45,092 --> 00:08:46,693
and your mother came,

268
00:08:46,693 --> 00:08:48,522
in that same brown coat.

269
00:08:48,702 --> 00:08:50,060
She sat in the back.

270
00:08:50,240 --> 00:08:51,552
She brought a pie.

271
00:08:51,732 --> 00:08:54,288
Then he took a little photograph out

272
00:08:54,288 --> 00:08:55,282
of his pocket,

273
00:08:55,282 --> 00:08:57,270
very creased, and showed me.

274
00:08:57,450 --> 00:08:59,999
A wedding couple on church steps,

275
00:08:59,999 --> 00:09:01,388
and in the corner,

276
00:09:01,388 --> 00:09:02,392
half cut off,

277
00:09:02,392 --> 00:09:04,245
a woman in a brown coat,

278
00:09:04,245 --> 00:09:05,635
holding a pie tin.

279
00:09:05,985 --> 00:09:06,834
But the why,

280
00:09:06,834 --> 00:09:09,449
honey, I didn't learn until after the

281
00:09:09,449 --> 00:09:10,014
funeral.

282
00:09:10,194 --> 00:09:13,154
When I cleaned out my mother's dresser,

283
00:09:13,154 --> 00:09:15,432
I found an old Christmas card,

284
00:09:15,432 --> 00:09:17,329
from nineteen thirty-two,

285
00:09:17,329 --> 00:09:19,758
very worn, with a note inside in

286
00:09:19,758 --> 00:09:22,187
her handwriting from much later.

287
00:09:22,367 --> 00:09:25,457
It said, the Christmas I was twelve,

288
00:09:25,457 --> 00:09:27,860
Papa had lost his job at the

289
00:09:27,860 --> 00:09:30,692
rubber plant, and we had nothing.

290
00:09:30,872 --> 00:09:32,646
So we went to the soup line

291
00:09:32,646 --> 00:09:33,762
on Fourth Street.

292
00:09:33,942 --> 00:09:36,044
Papa wouldn't lift his head.

293
00:09:36,224 --> 00:09:37,605
He was so ashamed.

294
00:09:37,956 --> 00:09:40,056
It said, and then a lady in

295
00:09:40,056 --> 00:09:42,235
a fur coat came in and stood

296
00:09:42,235 --> 00:09:45,036
behind the counter and handed us our

297
00:09:45,036 --> 00:09:46,359
plates, and said,

298
00:09:46,359 --> 00:09:48,927
Merry Christmas, you poor things.

299
00:09:49,107 --> 00:09:51,081
And Papa stopped eating.

300
00:09:51,261 --> 00:09:53,336
And then a different lady,

301
00:09:53,336 --> 00:09:55,332
in an old coat like ours,

302
00:09:55,332 --> 00:09:57,089
sat down at our table.

303
00:09:57,269 --> 00:09:59,022
She ate the same soup.

304
00:09:59,202 --> 00:10:01,575
She asked Papa about his work and

305
00:10:01,575 --> 00:10:03,660
told him he had clever hands.

306
00:10:03,840 --> 00:10:05,923
And Papa lifted his head,

307
00:10:05,923 --> 00:10:07,172
and he laughed.

308
00:10:07,352 --> 00:10:09,917
I hadn't heard him laugh in a

309
00:10:09,917 --> 00:10:10,359
year.

310
00:10:10,710 --> 00:10:12,125
And at the bottom,

311
00:10:12,125 --> 00:10:13,855
my mother had written,

312
00:10:13,855 --> 00:10:15,899
I promised God that night,

313
00:10:15,899 --> 00:10:17,629
if we ever had enough,

314
00:10:17,629 --> 00:10:19,673
I would be the lady in the

315
00:10:19,673 --> 00:10:20,381
old coat.

316
00:10:20,561 --> 00:10:23,115
Never the lady in the fur.

317
00:10:23,295 --> 00:10:25,464
I sat on the floor of her

318
00:10:25,464 --> 00:10:28,415
bedroom, honey, holding that card.

319
00:10:28,595 --> 00:10:31,045
And I understood the brown coat.

320
00:10:31,225 --> 00:10:33,848
And the torn pocket she never fixed.

321
00:10:34,028 --> 00:10:36,211
And especially today.

322
00:10:36,391 --> 00:10:39,758
She'd been keeping that promise for thirty-five

323
00:10:39,758 --> 00:10:40,188
years.

324
00:10:40,368 --> 00:10:42,573
And she never told a soul.

325
00:10:42,924 --> 00:10:46,030
That Christmas, nineteen eighty-nine,

326
00:10:46,030 --> 00:10:47,457
at three o'clock,

327
00:10:47,457 --> 00:10:49,639
I stood up from the table,

328
00:10:49,639 --> 00:10:50,563
sweetheart.

329
00:10:50,743 --> 00:10:52,769
I went to my own closet,

330
00:10:52,769 --> 00:10:55,471
and I took out my mother's brown

331
00:10:55,471 --> 00:10:57,244
coat, which I'd kept.

332
00:10:57,424 --> 00:10:59,003
And her gray scarf.

333
00:10:59,183 --> 00:11:01,240
And Harold looked at me,

334
00:11:01,240 --> 00:11:02,783
and he didn't ask.

335
00:11:02,963 --> 00:11:05,215
He just went and got his own

336
00:11:05,215 --> 00:11:06,341
oldest jacket.

337
00:11:06,521 --> 00:11:08,553
And we walked down to St.

338
00:11:08,733 --> 00:11:10,254
Vincent's together.

339
00:11:10,434 --> 00:11:11,935
And Eddie was there,

340
00:11:11,935 --> 00:11:13,812
at a table by the window.

341
00:11:13,992 --> 00:11:15,885
He'd saved two seats.

342
00:11:16,235 --> 00:11:19,937
Harold and I went every Christmas afternoon

343
00:11:19,937 --> 00:11:21,486
after that, honey,

344
00:11:21,486 --> 00:11:22,864
until he passed.

345
00:11:23,044 --> 00:11:24,710
Then I went with Susan,

346
00:11:24,710 --> 00:11:26,666
then with my grandchildren.

347
00:11:26,846 --> 00:11:29,435
Eddie came until nineteen ninety-six.

348
00:11:29,615 --> 00:11:30,196
St.

349
00:11:30,376 --> 00:11:33,190
Vincent's is a different building now,

350
00:11:33,190 --> 00:11:34,671
bigger and brighter.

351
00:11:34,851 --> 00:11:36,637
But every Christmas Day,

352
00:11:36,637 --> 00:11:40,132
at three-thirty, somebody from our family walks

353
00:11:40,132 --> 00:11:41,843
in wearing an old coat,

354
00:11:41,843 --> 00:11:42,810
gets a plate,

355
00:11:42,810 --> 00:11:45,562
and sits down with whoever is sitting

356
00:11:45,562 --> 00:11:46,009
alone.

357
00:11:46,189 --> 00:11:48,627
My granddaughter Lily goes now.

358
00:11:48,807 --> 00:11:50,920
She wears the gray scarf.

359
00:11:51,270 --> 00:11:54,015
Here's what I want you to remember,

360
00:11:54,015 --> 00:11:54,486
honey.

361
00:11:54,666 --> 00:11:56,964
Don't stand behind the counter.

362
00:11:57,144 --> 00:11:58,630
Sit down at the table.

363
00:11:58,810 --> 00:12:02,096
That's the difference between charity and love.

364
00:12:02,276 --> 00:12:03,973
Tell me in the comments,

365
00:12:03,973 --> 00:12:06,871
sweetheart, did someone in your family do

366
00:12:06,871 --> 00:12:08,638
something kind in secret?

367
00:12:08,818 --> 00:12:11,053
There are more of my family's stories

368
00:12:11,053 --> 00:12:13,167
in the playlist right there on your

369
00:12:13,167 --> 00:12:13,590
screen.

370
00:12:13,770 --> 00:12:14,583
Good night.

371
00:12:14,763 --> 00:12:16,226
Merry Christmas.
